<DocsLayout title="Notifications" activeNav="/platform/integrations/notifications">
  <h1>Notifications</h1>
  <p>
    Notification destinations turn platform events into messages in a chat channel. Unlike webhooks,
    which hand a payload to your own code, this is the built-in path: pick the events, write the
    message once, and route it to Slack, Discord, or Telegram.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="integrations-notifications"
    alt="Notifications tab with two destinations, provider selection, channel label, stored webhook URL, notification tags and per-event toggles"
    caption="The routing model: event, then template, then destination."
  />

  <h2>Event, template, destination</h2>
  <p>
    The three chips in the header describe the whole model. A <strong>destination</strong> subscribes
    to <strong>events</strong>, and each subscribed event has its own <strong>template</strong> — the
    message body that gets sent. Nothing is delivered for an event with no template, which is why the
    events list and the templates are edited together on one screen.
  </p>

  <h2>Providers</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Provider</th><th>What it needs</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Slack</td><td>An incoming webhook URL. The webhook itself determines the channel.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Discord</td><td>A channel webhook URL. Messages are capped at 2,000 characters.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Telegram</td><td>A bot token plus a numeric chat ID or an <code>@channel</code> username.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <strong>Channel label</strong> is only a recognisable name for the destination — it does not
    route anything. For Slack and Discord the webhook URL decides where messages land, so a label of
    <code>#mobile-releases</code> pointing at a webhook for <code>#general</code> will mislead you and
    nothing else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Credentials are encrypted at rest and never shown again. The field reads
    <em>Stored securely — enter a new URL to replace it</em>, and leaving it blank keeps the current
    value rather than clearing it, the same convention as the credentials vault.
  </p>

  <h2>Templates</h2>
  <p>
    Each event's message is a template with double-braced, dotted placeholders:
  </p>
  <pre><code>&#123;&#123;build.status_icon&#125;&#125; &#123;&#123;app.name&#125;&#125; &#123;&#123;build.profile&#125;&#125; build &#123;&#123;build.status&#125;&#125; in &#123;&#123;build.duration&#125;&#125; — &#123;&#123;build.url&#125;&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Which placeholders are available depends on the event. Build events expose the build; artifact
    events expose the artifact; identifier events expose the identifier.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Group</th><th>Placeholders</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Application</td><td><code>app.name</code></td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Build</td>
        <td>
          <code>build.number</code>, <code>build.profile</code>, <code>build.status</code>,
          <code>build.status_icon</code>, <code>build.duration</code>, <code>build.branch</code>,
          <code>build.requested_by</code>, <code>build.url</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Identifier</td>
        <td>
          <code>identifier.name</code>, <code>identifier.value</code>,
          <code>identifier.platform</code>, <code>identifier.version</code>,
          <code>identifier.url</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Artifact</td>
        <td>
          <code>artifact.name</code>, <code>artifact.type</code>, <code>artifact.size</code>,
          <code>artifact.download_url</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td>Tags</td><td><code>tags</code>, <code>tags_str</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A placeholder with no value renders as <code>—</code> rather than an empty gap, so a message
    stays readable when a build has no branch recorded.
  </p>

  <h3>Notification tags</h3>
  <p>
    Tags added to a destination are inserted into templates through <code>&#123;&#123;tags&#125;&#125;</code>
    or <code>&#123;&#123;tags_str&#125;&#125;</code>. They are a property of the destination, not of the
    build — useful for marking which channel or environment a message came from.
  </p>

  <h2>Events and the Active toggle</h2>
  <p>
    Each event has its own toggle, and the badge beside it — <strong>Will send</strong> — confirms the
    combination of subscription and template is complete. The <strong>Active</strong> switch at the
    top disables the whole destination without deleting it or losing its templates, which is what you
    want during a noisy incident.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with webhooks, there is no <code>build.failed</code> event: subscribe to
    <code>build.completed</code> and use <code>&#123;&#123;build.status&#125;&#125;</code> in the
    template, which is exactly what the seeded example above does.
  </p>

  <h2>Failures</h2>
  <p>
    Destinations track consecutive failures and the last delivery time, and those changes show up in
    the <a href="/platform/audit-log">audit log</a> — the entries reading
    <code>Failures: 2 → 3</code> come from here. A destination whose webhook URL was revoked in Slack
    keeps failing silently otherwise, so that counter is the signal worth watching.
  </p>

  <h2>Console-only, by omission rather than by design</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Notification destinations have no API.</strong> Their routes live in the
    session-authenticated web application, and <code>routes/api.php</code> defines nothing for them,
    so there is no API v1 endpoint and no CLI command — not even through
    <code>appmctl request</code>, which is scoped to <code>/api/v1/&#123;team&#125;/</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need chat notifications under version control or created by a script, use
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">webhooks</a> and post to chat from your own receiver.
    That trades the built-in templates for control over both the message and where its configuration
    lives. The same point, from the CLI's side, is on
    <a href="/guides/cli/integrations/notifications">the CLI notifications page</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
